          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em Lawvere theory
            is a joint generalization of the notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
          %p
            In his
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Functorial+Semantics+of+Algebraic+Theories"} 1963 doctoral dissertation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
            (with generating operations satisfying equational axioms). The rough idea is to define an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with finite
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            (e.g., a group) as a product-preserving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            out of that
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %em Lawvere algebraic theory
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p There are two related definitions of Lawvere theory in the literature. Both definitions are widely used and both have a substantial history.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_a Definition A
            %p
              A
              %strong
                Lawvere
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              or
              %strong finite-product theory
              is (equivalently encoded by its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
              which is) a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              with finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              in which every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to a finite cartesian power
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ×
                    %mi x
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ×
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n = x \times x \times \cdots \times x
              of a distinguished object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              (called the
              %em generic object
              for the theory
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of such theories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to T'
              is a product-preserving
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              which also preserves the generic object.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_b Definition B
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔽
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}
              be the category of natural numbers and functions between them (a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
              of the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} category of finite sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            %p
              A
              %strong
                Lawvere
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              equipped with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects
              strictly
              = succeed "-preserving" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power"} power
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J:\mathbb{F}^{\mathrm{op}} \to T
          %p In this definition, a homomorphism is a product-preserving functor making the obvious triangle commute strictly.
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Clearly, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is part of a structure in the form of Definition B, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is of the form in Definition A, taking
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=J(1)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              a Lawvere theory, we are to think of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n,1) := T(x^n, x)
              as the set of
              = succeed "-ary" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              the theory of
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(2,1)
              includes operations like
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} + \colon (x, y) \mapsto x + y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \colon (x, y) \mapsto x-y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mn 2
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mn 3
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y) \mapsto 2 x - 3 y
              = succeed "-ary" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(0,1) = \{0\}
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              – an
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+Lawvere+theory"} algebra over the Lawvere theory
              or simply
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                \-algebra
              – is a product-preserving
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A : T \to Set
                    \\,,
            %p
              and
              %em
                homomorphism of
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              between such functors.
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Such a functor picks out a single
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(A) := A(1)
              and picks for every
              = succeed "-ary" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in T(n,1)
              an actual
              = succeed "-ary" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A(\phi) : U(A)^n \to U(A)
            %p
              such that these operations are all compatible with each other in the way governed by the composition rules of morphisms in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %p
              When working with Definition B of Lawvere theory, it would be normal to require that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %em strictly
              preserves products.
          %h3#remarks Remarks
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                From some perspectives, Definition B violates the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clone"} abstract clone
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monad"} relative monad
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔽
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                for each natural number, and thus derive an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
                product-preserving functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝔽
                          %mi op
                        %mo →
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}^{op} \to T
            %li
              %p
                Some people use ‘finite-product theory’ to mean any (small) category with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                can be regarded as a special case of
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/toddtrimble/published/multisorted+Lawvere+theories"} multisorted Lawvere theories
                (see below) where the set of sorts is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(C)
                itself. Some, but not all, the above discussion generalizes to this case.
            %li
              %p
                As
                = succeed "," do
                  %em finite-product theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theories
                of differing logical strengths. For example, there are left exact theories,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a category with finite products, then a group (object) in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                may be defined as a product-preserving functor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi Grp
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp} \to C
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi Grp
                        %mo →
                        %mi Top
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp} \to Top
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi Grp
                      %mo →
                      %mi Man
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp} \to Man
                into the category of smooth
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %h3#variations Variations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong multisorted
                or multityped Lawvere theory for a given set of sorts
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is a category with finite products
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                together with a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: S \to Ob(C)
                such that every object of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is isomorphic to a finite product of objects of the form
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(S)
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+Lawvere+theory"} infinitary Lawvere theory
                allows for infinitary operations. An example is the theory of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suplattices"} suplattices
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                elements. While Lawvere theories correspond to finitary monads on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat+theory"} Fermat theory
                is a Lawvere theory equipped with a notion of differentiation.
            %li
              %p
                A finite-product theory can also be presented without including all the products of the basic types as actual objects. This yields the notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+multicategory"} cartesian multicategory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#TheoryOfSets The theory of sets
          %p
            The tautological example of a Lawvere theory is the algebraic theory of
            %em no
            operations. This is also called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong theory of equality
          %p
            Its syntactic category is the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            on objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            with morphisms generated by the projections
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i : n \to 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mi op
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{S} \simeq FinSet^{op}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            in the category of Lawvere theories.
          %p
            An algebra over this theory is just a bare
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{S} Alg \simeq Set
                  \\,.
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            any Lawvere theory, there is a canonical morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S} \to T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U_T : T Alg \to \mathcal{S}Alg \simeq Set
                  \\,.
          %p
            This sends each algebra to its
            %em underlying set
            \. For more on this see the section
            %a{:href => "#FreeAlgebras"} Free T-algebras
            below.
          %h3#the_theory_of_groups The theory of groups
          %p
            We consider here the theory of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n)
            (for any natural number
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            generators, and define the Lawvere theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            to be the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
            to the category of free groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n)
            and group homomorphisms. The generic object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            is taken to be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1)
          %p
            The category of free groups has finite coproducts since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo +
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(m) + F(n) \cong F(m+n)
            (in other words, the inclusion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FreeGroup
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Group
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FreeGroup \hookrightarrow Group
          %p
            creates coproducts in
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FreeGroup
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FreeGroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            has finite products, and we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n) = x^n
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            defines a product-preserving functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi FreeGroup
                    %mi op
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi Group
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp} = FreeGroup^{op} \hookrightarrow Group^{op} \stackrel{\hom(-, G)}{\to} Set
          %p
            since contravariant hom-functors take coproducts to products. Thus any group gives a model of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
          %p The other direction is more interesting. Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M: T_{Grp} \to Set
          %p
            be a model of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = M(x)
          %p
            To understand this, let’s consider how group multiplication would be defined. The idea is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            is a “generic group”, so we first need to understand how multiplication works there. The idea is that the product in the generic group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m: x^2 \to x^1
          %p corresponds to a homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1) \to F(2)
          %p
            which by freeness corresponds to an element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to F(2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b
            of the generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
            of the free group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(2) = F(a, b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
            themselves correspond to the two coproduct inclusions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_1: F(1) \to F(1) + F(1) = F(2) \qquad i_2: F(1) \to F(1) + F(1) = F(2)
          %p
            Then, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is assumed to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} preserve products
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \times G = M(x) \times M(x) \cong M(x^2) \stackrel{M(m)}{\to} M(x) = G
          %p
            and this defines the group multiplication on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            are defined by following similar recipes.
          %p
            It may be checked that the notion of homomorphism of
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{Grp}
          %p In particular, any hom-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi Grp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_{T_{Grp}}(x^n, -): T_{Grp} \to Set
          %p
            preserves products, and so defines a group. This group is precisely the free group on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            generators, and a little thought shows that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            generators correspond to the natural transformations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi Grp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi Grp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_{T_{Grp}}(x, -) \to \hom_{T_{Grp}}(x^n, -)
          %p
            induced by the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            projection maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n \to x
          %p
            All of the discussion above for the case of groups generalizes to any finitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            (i.e., any single-sorted theory whose signature consists of function symbols of finite arity, subject to universally quantified equational axioms). In summary:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The Lawvere theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                is the category opposite to the category of free algebras on finitely many generators,
            %li
              %p
                The category of algebras is in turn equivalent to the category of product-preserving functors
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to Set
            %li
              %p
                The free algebras are retrieved as the representable functors
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to Set
          %p
            As discussed in the article on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operads
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal categories
          %h3#OtherExamples Other examples
          %p
            Most of the standard structures that are considered in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            indeed are models of algebraic/Lawvere theories in the precise sense. The following list gives a few familiar examples and a few not so familiar ones, but there are many more. Beware though that there are also some familiar examples that seem to be algebraic but are not, these we discuss
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SomeNonExamples"} below
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                %em
                  free
                  = succeed "-associative" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                is the (syntactic category of the) theory of ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                a fixed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+representations"}> permutation representations
                ) are an example of algebras over a Lawvere theory
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                a fixed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                are an example
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                are an example
            %li
              %p
                The category of say
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattices"} distributive lattices
                is the category of algebras of a Lawvere theory. So is the category of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebras"} Heyting algebras
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
                is the (syntactic category of the) theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebras"} smooth algebras
                (as used in
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat+theory"} Fermat theory
            %li
              %p
                The terminal Lawvere theory has exactly one morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi m
                      %mo →
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon m \to n
                for each
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,n
          %h3#CharacterizationOfExamples Characterization of examples
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature"} signature
            consisting of finitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operations"} operations
            (but no
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sort"} sort
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            all of which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universally quantified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
            between terms, then a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            can be described as an algebra of a Lawvere theory.
          %p
            This includes most cases arising in a typical undergraduate course in modern algebra, as the examples
            %a{:href => "#OtherExamples"} above
            suggest.
          %p
            There are also well-known criteria for a category of single-sorted structures
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: C \to Set
          #RecognizingAConcreteCategoryAsAlgebrasOverALawvereTheory.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \colon C \to Set
              is a category of algebras over a Lawvere theory precisely if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic
              %li
                %p
                  is
                  %em finitary
                  in that it preserves
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimits"} filtered colimits
          %p Another characterization is:
          #BirkhoffHSP.num_example
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birkhoff%27s+HSP+theorem"}> Birkhoff's HSP theorem
                )
            %p
              Suppose given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language"} language
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              generated by a set of (single-sorted) finitary operations, and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is the class of models for a set of universally quantified equations between terms of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              if and only if
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (H) The class is closed under homomorphic
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
              %li
                %p (S) The class is closed under subalgebras,
              %li
                %p (P) The class is closed under taking products.
          %h3#SomeNonExamples Some non-examples
          %p Here are some notable examples of mathematical structures that look algebraic, but are not models of an algebraic theory in the present sense:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The class of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
                is not the class of algebras of a Lawvere theory.
            %li
              %p
                Neither is the class of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domains"} integral domains
          %p
            This might seem obvious since multiplicative inversion in fields is not a global operation, or otherwise the cancellation law of multiplication in integral domains is not a universally quantified axiom (since we have to make an exception of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %p
            The first clause in Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RecognizingAConcreteCategoryAsAlgebrasOverALawvereTheory"}
            already rules out fields, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            in that case will create
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Field"} Field
            of fields does not even have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domains"} integral domains
          %p
            The second clause in Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RecognizingAConcreteCategoryAsAlgebrasOverALawvereTheory"}
            suggests another type of non-example:
          %ul
            %li
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SLat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SLat
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sup-lattices"} sup-lattices
              is not the category of algebras of a (finitary) Lawvere theory.
          %p
            There is a whole class of infinitary sup-operations for sup-lattices (one for every arity = cardinal), but again one may wonder how one rules out any alternative finitary axiomatizations. But this is fairly clear by invoking the second clause and considering the following example: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi SLat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: SLat \to Set
            created filtered colimits, then the countable copower
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \cdot \mathbf{2}
            of 2-element sup-lattices (which turns out to be the power set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathbb{N})
            with its usual order) would be the filtered colimit (in fact a union) over finite subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of finite copowers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \cdot \mathbf{2}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#coequalizers_of_algebras
            Coequalizers of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .un_def
            %h6#congruence Definition (congruence)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be a Lawvere theory and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "-algebra." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence"} congruence
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              on the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(1)
              such that whenever for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              whenever any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_i \in A(1))_{i=1}^n
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b_i \in A(1))_{i=1}^n
              are pairwise equivalent,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∼
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i \sim b_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in T(n,1)
              the results are equivalent:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a_1, \cdots, a_n) \sim f(b_1, \cdots , b_n)
          .un_lemma
            %h6#observationdefinition Observation/Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subset A(1) \times A(1)
              there is a smallest
              %a{:href => "#congruence"} congruence
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              containing
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle R \rangle \subset A(1) \times A(1)
              for this smallest congruence.
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a congruence on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi C
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/C(f) \subset (A/C)^n \times A/C
              induced by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(f)
              for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \in T(n,1)
              are functions and define a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(1)/C
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g : A \to B
              two morphisms of
              = succeed "-algebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to B/\langle \f(a)\sim g(a) | a \in A(1)\rangle
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %h3#FreeAlgebras
            Free
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            for the (syntactic category of the) algebraic theory of sets (described
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#TheoryOfSets"} above
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            any other (syntactic category of a) Lawvere theory, there is a canonical morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i_T :  \mathcal{S} \to T
                  \\,.
          %p
            By precomposition with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_T
            we obtain a corresponding functor on
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U_T : T Alg \to Set
          %p
            and call the
            %strong underlying set
            functor.
          .un_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_T
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi Set
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mi Alg
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_T : Set \to T Alg
          %p
            This is a standard example of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+functor"} free functor
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                free
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_T(S)
              be the
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f(s_1, \cdots, s_n) | f \in T(n,1), s_i \in S\}
              with the evident composition operation.
          .un_lemma
            %h6#observation Observation
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = (n) \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
              a
              %em finite set
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements , the free
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_T(S) = T(n,-) : T \to Set
                    \\,.
            %p The adjunction isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T Alg(F_T(S), A) \simeq Set(S, U_T(A)) \simeq  (A(1))^n \simeq A(n)
            %p
              is in this case just the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T Alg(T(n,-),A) \simeq A(n)
                    \\,.
            %p Notice that this extends to a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_T(-) : FinSet \to T Alg
            %p which is the composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi i
                          %mi T
                          %mi op
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi j
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_T(-) : FinSet \stackrel{i_T^{op}}{\to} T^{op}
                    \\stackrel{j}{\to}
                    T Alg
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
              with the opposite of the canonical functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi FinSet
                      %mi op
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_T : FinSet^{op} \simeq \mathcal{S} \to T
              from the theory of sets, described
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#TheoryOfSets"} above
            %p
              More generally, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in Set
              not necessarily finite, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(S)
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              of finite subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and their inclusions.
            %p
              Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_T(S)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered colimit
              of the representables corresponding to the finite subsets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mi lim
                          %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Sub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_T(S) \simeq {\lim_{\to}}_{{n \in Sub(S)}} T(n,-)
                    \\,.
            %p
              As discussed
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#CoLimitsOfTAlgebras"} below
              = succeed "-algebras" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %h3#AdjCatAlgs
            Adjunctions of relatively free
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p The following establishes that more generally any morphism of Lawvere theories leads to an adjunction between their categories of algebras.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            be Lawvere theories and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : T_1 \to T_2
            a morphism. Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* : T_2 Alg \to T_1 Alg
            for the functor on categories of algebras induced by precomposition with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .un_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 1
                      %mi Alg
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                      %mi Alg
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_* : T_1 Alg \to T_2 Alg
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p Here is an elementary proof:
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi i
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{T_i} \dashv U_{T_i}
              be the two
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#FreeAlgebras"} free algebra/underlying-set adjunctions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
              %a{:href => "#congruence"} congruence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Γ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \simeq F_{T_1} U_{T_1}(A) / \Gamma
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since for any set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{T_1} F_{T_1}(S) \subset U_{T_2} F_{T_2}(S)
              it follows that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \subset T_{T_2} F_{T_2} U_{T_1}(A)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \Gamma \rangle
              the smallest
              = succeed "-congruence" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{T_2} U_{T_1}(A) / \langle \Gamma \rangle
              is a
              = succeed "-algebra." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            %p
              This one checks is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_* A
            %p
              Here is a more high-powered way to obtain this using the
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i
              whose algebras are
              = succeed "-algebras:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_i
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo ∘
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_* A  := K_2 \circ_{K_1} A
              the the evident reflexive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 1
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo ∘
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K_2 K_1 A \stackrel{\to}{\to} K_2 A \to K_2 \circ_{K_1} A
            %p
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                      %mi Alg
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2 Alg
          %h3#CoLimitsOfTAlgebras
            Limits and colimits of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              a Lawvere theory, the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg
              has all small
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %p
              The
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered colimit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg
              are computed pointwise.
          %p
            The category of algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+object"} zero object
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has a single nullary operation (Proposition 3.3 of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Wraith"} Wraith
          %h2#an_open_problem An open problem
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higman%27s+embedding+theorem"} famous result by G. Higman
            in group theory says that a finitely generated group can be embedded in a finitely presented group precisely if it has a presentation whose defining relations are a recursively enumerable set of words. Clearly, this question can be asked for every similar algebraic theory and it has been in fact conjectured by the group theorist W. Boone that the same result holds more generally for every single-sorted algebraic theory. While in fact the conjecture is not true for all single-sorted algebraic theories (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boone+conjecture"} Boone conjecture
            for further details), as was known by Soviet mathematicians, the general question “In which algebraic categories does (an analogue of) Higman’s embedding theorem hold?” is still interesting. In fact, it has been urged by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"} F. W. Lawvere
            on several occasions that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boone+conjecture"} Boone conjecture
            should be settled by the category theory community.
