          %h2#references References
          %p
            The origin of the categorical formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
            as Lawvere theories is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Functorial+Semantics+of+Algebraic+Theories"} Functorial Semantics of Algebraic Theories
              %strong 5
              (2004) pp 1–121. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/5/tr5abs.html"}> tac:tr5
              )
          %p The concept was then streamlined in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
              , J. B. Wright,
              %em Automata in General Algebras
              , Information and Control
              %strong 11
              (1967) pp.452-470.
          %p Also still worthwhile reading are the following early papers:
          %ul
            %li#Wraith
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"}> Gavin Wraith
                ,
                %em Algebraic Theories
                , Aarhus Lecture Notes Series no.22 (1969). (
                %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/ohad.kammar/scans/wraith-algebraic-theories.pdf"}> pdf, 3,7MB
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"}> Gavin Wraith
                ,
                %em Algebras over Theories
                , Colloquium Mathematicum
                %strong XXIII
                no.2 (1971) pp.181-190. (
                %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/264519"}> link
                )
          %p An early textbook treatment was in
          %ul
            %li#Pareigis70
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bodo+Pareigis"}> Bodo Pareigis
              ,
              %em Categories and Functors
              , New York: Academic Press 1970. (chap. 3;
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://epub.ub.uni-muenchen.de/7244/"} link
          %p Modern textbook treatments are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                %em Handbook of categorical algebra 2 – Categories and structures
                , Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Applications, Cambridge UP 1994. (chap. 3)
            %li
              %p
                M. C. Pedicchio, F. Rovatti,
                %em Algebraic Categories
                , pp.269-310 in Pedicchio, Tholen (eds.),
                %em Categorical Foundations
                , Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 97
          %p A recent monograph is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Adamek"}> J. Adámek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"} J. Rosicky
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Vitale"} E. M. Vitale
              %em Algebraic Theories - a Categorical Introduction to General Algebra
              , Cambrige UP 2010. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.iti.cs.tu-bs.de/~adamek/algebraic_theories.pdf"}> draft
              )
          %p The concept of an internal algebraic theory in topos theory is dicussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"} Gavin Wraith
                %em Algebraic theories in toposes
                , pp.141-242 in LNM
                %strong 661
                Springer Heidelberg 1978.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em Topos Theory
                , Academic Press New York 1977. (Dover reprint 2014; sec. 6.4, pp.190-198)
          %p
            Comparison with the notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"}> Martin Hyland
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"} John Power
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Category Theoretic Understanding of Universal Algebra: Lawvere Theories and Monads
              %strong 172
              (2007) 437-458 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2007.02.019"}> doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2007.02.019
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~jmeh1/Research/Publications/2007/hp07.pdf"} preprint
          %p
            An elementary proof of the equivalence between infinitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            is given in Appendix A of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Brandenburg"}> Martin Brandenburg
              , Large limit sketches and topological space objects,
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.11115"} arXiv:2106.11115
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+law"} Distributive laws
            for algebraic theories are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenia+Cheng"}> Eugenia Cheng
              ,
              %em Distributive laws for Lawvere theories
              , arXiv:1112.3076 (2011). (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3076"}> abstract
              )
          %p
            Voevodsky proves an equivalence between Lawvere theories and l-bijective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-systems"} C-systems
            here:
          %ul
            %li#Voevodsky
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lawvere theories and C-systems
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1512.08104"} abstract
          %p Other references are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                M. Jibladze, T. Pirashvili,
                %em Cohomology of Algebraic Theories
                , J. Algebra
                %strong 137
                no.2 (1991) pp.253–296.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"}> Steve Lack
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"} Jiri Rosicky
                %em Notions of Lawvere theory
                , arXiv:0810.2578. (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.2578"}> abstract
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Vitale"}> Enrico Vitale
                ,
                %em Localization of Algebraic Categories
                , JPAA
                %strong 108
                (1996) pp.315-320.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Vitale"}> Enrico Vitale
                ,
                %em Localization of Algebraic Categories 2
                , JPAA
                %strong 133
                (1998) pp.317-326.
          %p
            Lawvere theories, and finitary monads, are identified with certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            and the passage from one to the other with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocompletion"} cocompletion
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"}> Richard Garner
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lawvere theories, finitary monads and Cauchy-completion
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.2963"} arxiv
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"} John Power
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An enriched view on the extended finitary monad–Lawvere theory correspondence
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.08694"} arxiv
          %p Some properties of Lawvere theories:
          %ul
            %li
              V. Trnková,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Representability and local representability of algebraic theories
          %p A short introductory/overview note:
          %ul
            %li#keml-diagrams
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Nuyts"}> Andreas Nuyts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Understanding Universal Algebra Using Kleisli-Eilenberg-Moore-Lawvere Diagrams
              %a{:href => "https://anuyts.github.io/files/keml-diagrams.pdf"} note
